=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The inter-AS physical link between router1 and router2 is fully operational — both
routers have 193.10.11.0/24 showing as directly connected on eth0. The team's
re-cabling hypothesis is incorrect.

The real fault is that the bgpd daemon on router1 has stopped running. On router1,
only zebra, mgmtd, and staticd appear in the process list; bgpd is absent. Despite
this, /etc/frr/daemons correctly has bgpd=yes, and /etc/frr/frr.conf contains a
complete, valid eBGP configuration: AS 1, neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2,
advertising 195.11.14.0/24. Because bgpd is dead, router1 never opens a TCP
session on port 179, so router2's bgpd keeps the neighbor 193.10.11.1 permanently
in the "Active" state — perpetually retrying with no response. Neither router
installs the other's prefix via BGP, leaving router1 with no route to 200.1.1.0/24
and router2 with no route to 195.11.14.0/24. Consequently pc1 (195.11.14.2)
cannot reach pc2 (200.1.1.2) and vice versa.

Root cause: the bgpd process on router1 crashed or was killed, taking down the
only eBGP session that provides cross-AS reachability.

Corrective fix: restart the FRR service on router1 (run `service frr restart` or
`systemctl restart frr` inside the router1 container). This will re-launch bgpd,
which will load the existing frr.conf, establish the eBGP session with router2,
exchange the two prefixes, and restore full pc1 ↔ pc2 connectivity. No physical
re-cabling is needed.
=== END ===
